          "Atomicity means a group of writes either all happen or none happen. Moving money from account A to account B is 2 writes: debit A, then credit B. If the process crashes between those 2 writes, atomicity rolls the database back to before either one happened. Without it, you could end up with the debit applied and no matching credit, meaning the money is just gone.",
          "Isolation means 2 transactions running at once can't see each other's half-finished work. Say a joint account holds $1,000, and 2 people each try to withdraw $700 from it within the same second. Both transactions start by reading the balance. Without isolation, both could read $1,000, both do the math on that same stale number, and both succeed, paying out $1,400 total from an account that only had $1,000. With isolation, the second transaction is blocked from reading the balance until the first one finishes committing its withdrawal. It then reads the true, updated $300, correctly fails the check, and the account never goes negative.",
